# ==========================================================================
#  O15  An IRI is not a name
# ==========================================================================
#
#  DECIDES
#    The IRI identifies and the label names; a shop can be renamed without
#    becoming a different shop, and an IRI may be readable or opaque as
#    long as nothing depends on reading it.
#
#  WHY
#    The Inkwell might change its name to The Wigtown Inkwell next year. If
#    the IRI were the name, every link to the shop would break, and every
#    dataset that mentioned it would need editing. Because the IRI is
#    bt:shop-inkwell and the name is a label, the label changes and nothing
#    else does. The old name stays as an altLabel, so a search still finds
#    it. This is the practical content of the Web Architecture's URI
#    opacity: agents should not infer anything from the spelling of an IRI,
#    and so nothing should depend on the spelling staying true.
#
#    That said, people read IRIs, and the two big upper ontologies took
#    opposite views. gist writes gist:Content and gist:Organization,
#    readable at a glance. BFO writes obo:BFO_0000031 for 'generically
#    dependent continuant', a number that will never go stale because it
#    never said anything. gistBFO, the file that maps one to the other, has
#    both styles on every line. Neither is wrong. A readable IRI is kinder
#    to the person writing a query and a small liability the day the
#    meaning drifts from the word; an opaque one puts all the meaning in
#    the label and definition, where it can be edited. The OBO Foundry
#    requires the opaque form for its members and gist requires the
#    readable one; both forbid changing an IRI once published.
#
#    The Trail's IRIs are readable and lower-case, with a type prefix:
#    shop-inkwell, place-wigtown, author-rhona-blackwood. o20 compares that
#    with gist's convention. What matters here is only that the reader
#    knows which of the three things on screen is which: the IRI is the
#    identifier, the label is the name, the comment is the meaning.
#
#  DIAGRAM
#       the shop is renamed                     the IRI                  the label
#
#       before                                  bt:shop-inkwell          "The Inkwell"@en
#       after                                   bt:shop-inkwell          "The Wigtown Inkwell"@en
#                                                                        altLabel "The Inkwell"@en
#       every link still works; the search still finds the old name
#
#       readable                                opaque
#       gist:Content                            obo:BFO_0000031
#       the meaning is in the IRI and the       the meaning is in the label
#       label; drift is a small liability       and definition only; drift is free
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - The IRI identifies; the label names. Rename by changing the label
#      and keeping the old one as an alternative.
#    - Opacity is a rule for machines: infer nothing from the spelling.
#      It is also advice for modellers: depend on nothing in it.
#    - Readable and opaque IRIs are both respectable. Pick one convention
#      and never change a published IRI.
#
#  TRY IT
#    In the SPARQL panel, ask for every label of bt:shop-inkwell. Then
#    change the prefLabel in the editor, press Get All, and ask again: the
#    IRI in the answer has not moved.
#
#  CORNER    symbol
#  RUNS IN   the browser
#  LOAD IT   https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FOntology_Course%2Fmain%2Flessons%2F03-naming%2Fo15-an-iri-is-not-a-name.ttl
#  SOURCES   Architecture of the World Wide Web 2.5 URI Opacity
#            https://www.w3.org/TR/webarch/#uri-opacity
#            OBO Foundry principle FP-003, URI/identifier space
#            https://obofoundry.org/principles/fp-003-uris.html
#  MEASURED  parses, 16 triples; 3 classes, 1 individuals; OWL 2 DL in profile; 'Every name of the shop, with' 2 rows
# ==========================================================================

@prefix bt:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/> .
@prefix bs:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix gist: <https://w3id.org/semanticarts/ns/ontology/gist/> .
@prefix obo:  <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/> .

# Borrowed annotation properties, declared so the file is OWL 2 DL on its own.
skos:prefLabel      a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:altLabel       a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:definition     a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:editorialNote  a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:notation       a owl:AnnotationProperty .

bt:shop-inkwell
    a               bs:Bookshop ;
    skos:prefLabel  "The Wigtown Inkwell"@en ;
    skos:altLabel   "The Inkwell"@en ;
    skos:definition "The crime fiction shop on Wigtown's main street, opened in 1979. Renamed in 2026; the IRI did not change."@en .

bs:Bookshop
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Bookshop"@en .

# The two styles side by side, as gistBFO has them.
gist:Content
    a                owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel   "Content"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf  obo:BFO_0000031 .

obo:BFO_0000031
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "generically dependent continuant"@en .


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  AFTERWARDS.  Queries to run in the SPARQL panel, on this file (press
#  Add Prefixes first) or on the data file it names.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  -- Every name of the shop, with the IRI beside it  (on the model)
#  PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>
#  SELECT ?shop ?name WHERE { ?shop skos:prefLabel|skos:altLabel ?name . FILTER(CONTAINS(STR(?shop), "/shop-")) }
